{
  "title": "At the bar: ordering food and drinks",
  "title_target": "Tabernan",
  "goal": "Order drinks and pintxos, say what you want with nahi izan, and ask about prices.",
  "bekal": [
    "order with *… nahi dut, mesedez* and answer *zer nahi duzu?*",
    "name the drinks and pintxos you'll find in almost every Basque bar",
    "ask about and understand prices: *zenbat da?*"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "Elkarrizketa — Alde Zaharreko taberna batean",
      "intro": "Late afternoon in Bilbo's Old Town. Budi and Maite walk into a bar with pintxos laid out along the counter.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "Zerbitzaria:",
          "text": "Kaixo! Zer nahi duzue?",
          "id": "Hello! What would you like?",
          "literal": "hello! what want you(pl)-have-it?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Kafe huts bat nahi dut, mesedez.",
          "id": "A black coffee, please.",
          "literal": "coffee empty one want I-have-it, please."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Maite:",
          "text": "Niretzat kafesnea eta tortilla pintxo bat.",
          "id": "For me a white coffee and a tortilla pintxo.",
          "literal": "me-for coffee-milk-the and tortilla pintxo one."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Zerbitzaria:",
          "text": "Ondo. Zerbait gehiago?",
          "id": "Fine. Anything else?",
          "literal": "fine. something more?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Bai, pintxo bat niretzat ere. Zer duzue?",
          "id": "Yes, a pintxo for me too. What have you got?",
          "literal": "yes, pintxo one me-for also. what you(pl)-have-it?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Zerbitzaria:",
          "text": "Tortilla, gilda, bakailaoa eta txistorra ditugu.",
          "id": "We've got tortilla, gilda, salt cod, and txistorra sausage.",
          "literal": "tortilla, gilda, cod-the and txistorra-the we-have-them."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Barkatu, gilda zer da?",
          "id": "Sorry, what's a gilda?",
          "literal": "sorry, gilda what is?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Maite:",
          "text": "Piperra, oliba eta antxoa. Oso gozoa da!",
          "id": "Green pepper, olive, and anchovy. It's delicious!",
          "literal": "pepper-the, olive and anchovy. very tasty-the is!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Orduan, gilda bat, mesedez.",
          "id": "Then one gilda, please.",
          "literal": "then, gilda one, please."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Zerbitzaria:",
          "text": "Marchando! Hemen duzue.",
          "id": "Coming up! Here you are.",
          "literal": "coming-up! here you(pl)-have-it."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Eskerrik asko. Zenbat da dena?",
          "id": "Thank you. How much is everything?",
          "literal": "thanks many. how-much is all-the?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Zerbitzaria:",
          "text": "Zortzi euro eta hogei zentimo.",
          "id": "Eight euros twenty.",
          "literal": "eight euro and twenty cent."
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**A pintxo is not a tapa.** In Euskal Herria, bar snacks are called *pintxos* — from *pintxatu*, \"to pierce\", because they were originally speared onto a slice of bread with a toothpick. Unlike Spanish tapas, pintxos are almost always paid for (not free with your drink) and laid out on the counter so you can point. Pointing and saying *hori, mesedez* is a perfectly proper way to order.",
        "**Niretzat — the \"for\" ending.** *-entzat* means \"for\": *niretzat* (for me), *zuretzat* (for you), *Jonentzat* (for Jon), *guretzat* (for us). At the bar, *niretzat kafesnea* is the most natural way to order — even more common than a full sentence.",
        "**Nahi dut wants a thing — or a dictionary-form verb.** *Kafea nahi dut* (I want coffee). If what you want is an action, the verb goes in its dictionary form in front of *nahi*: *Kafea edan nahi dut* (I want to drink coffee) — literally \"coffee drink want I-have-it\", the reverse of the English order.",
        "**Marchando isn't Basque.** It's a Spanish word that every bar in the region has adopted — roughly \"coming right up!\". Bar talk is mixed; don't be surprised if the waiter answers you in euskara and then shouts to the kitchen in Spanish."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Standard phrases at the bar",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Euskara",
            "Say it",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Zer nahi duzu?",
              "sehr NAH-ee doo-soo",
              "What would you like?"
            ],
            [
              "Zer hartuko duzu?",
              "sehr ahr-TOO-koh doo-soo",
              "What will you have?"
            ],
            [
              "… nahi dut, mesedez.",
              "NAH-ee doot meh-SEH-dehs",
              "I'd like …, please."
            ],
            [
              "Niretzat …",
              "nee-REH-tsaht",
              "For me …"
            ],
            [
              "Hori, mesedez.",
              "OH-ree",
              "That one, please."
            ],
            [
              "Zerbait gehiago?",
              "sehr-BAH-eet geh-EE-ah-goh",
              "Anything else?"
            ],
            [
              "Ez, eskerrik asko.",
              "ehs ehs-KEH-rreek AHS-koh",
              "No, thank you."
            ],
            [
              "Zenbat da?",
              "sehn-BAHT dah",
              "How much is it?"
            ],
            [
              "Ordaindu nahi dut.",
              "ohr-DAH-een-doo",
              "I'd like to pay."
            ],
            [
              "Txartelarekin, mesedez.",
              "chahr-teh-LAH-reh-keen",
              "By card, please."
            ],
            [
              "On egin!",
              "ohn eh-GEEN",
              "Enjoy your meal!"
            ],
            [
              "Topa!",
              "TOH-pah",
              "Cheers!"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "*On egin* literally means \"may it do you good\" — said to someone who is eating, like \"enjoy your meal\" or *bon appétit*. If someone says it to you, just reply *baita zuri ere* (\"you too\")."
        },
        {
          "type": "kotak",
          "title": "Bar customs you should know",
          "items": [
            "In many bars you eat first and pay afterwards — on your honor. Say honestly how many pintxos you had.",
            "*Poteo* or *txikiteo* is the custom of moving from one bar to the next, one small glass at each, with your *kuadrilla* — a fixed group of friends who have usually been together since childhood.",
            "Small drinks have their own names: *zurito* (a small beer), *txikito* (a small glass of wine), *katxi* (a big plastic cup for festivals)."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
