        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "*Txakolina* and *sagardoa* are the region's two signature drinks. Txakoli is poured from a height so it fizzes; sagardo is drunk in a *sagardotegi* (cider house) straight from the barrel, to shouts of *txotx!* — the signal that the next barrel is being opened."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Nahi izan and behar izan",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "You'll use these two verbs every day, and they work exactly the same way: the thing or action first, *nahi/behar* in the middle, the auxiliary at the end."
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Euskara",
            "Literally",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Kafea **nahi dut**.",
              "coffee-the want I-have-it",
              "I want coffee."
            ],
            [
              "Kafea edan **nahi dut**.",
              "coffee-the drink want I-have-it",
              "I want to drink coffee."
            ],
            [
              "Etxera joan **nahi dut**.",
              "home-to go want I-have-it",
              "I want to go home."
            ],
            [
              "Ura **behar dut**.",
              "water-the need I-have-it",
              "I need water."
            ],
            [
              "Orain joan **behar dut**.",
              "now go need I-have-it",
              "I have to go now."
            ],
            [
              "**Ez dut** ezer nahi.",
              "not I-have-it anything want",
              "I don't want anything."
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Notice that the verb is in its **dictionary** form (*edan*, *joan*), not the *-t(z)en* form. The *-t(z)en* form is only for habits; after *nahi* and *behar* it's always the dictionary form."
        },
        {
          "type": "kotak",
          "title": "Paying and saying thanks",
          "items": [
            "*Zenbat da?* — how much is it. *Zenbat da dena?* if you ordered several things.",
            "*Hemen duzu* — \"here you are\" (when handing over money or something else).",
            "*Aldaketa* or *kanbioa* — change. *Ez daukat sentimorik* — \"I don't have any small change\".",
            "Tipping isn't expected in Euskal Herria; rounding up to the nearest euro is more than enough."
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
